    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Yeah, fluid looks okay, color is fine. No codes though, just the pressure spikes on the live data. I already looked at the solenoids and they seem to be responding to commands, but the actual pressure is just all over the place. Could it be something internal like the valve body or maybe a seal failing? I'm trying to figure out if I need to pull the whole unit or if there's a simpler fix.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Given that the solenoids are responding and the fluid is clear, the issue might indeed be within the valve body or seals, as you suggested. Minor valve body part failures can cause such symptoms without completely losing pressure. Check the condition of the valve body and its seals for possible leakage or damage. It's less likely you'll need to remove the transmission to catch the leak, but this could also be the case if the problem is with the PCV (Pressure Control Valve) or flexplate. If there's a visible leak or irregular movement, you might find something internally, but be cautious, as this indicates more extensive repairs may be needed. If the problem isn't obvious, then a transmission catch might not be necessary just yet.",
