    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "If it's the valve body, is there anything else that could cause those sudden spikes that wouldn't necessarily throw a sensor code? Like, is there any way it could be some kind of weird mechanical interference or just something just \"acting up\" randomly? It's driving me crazy because it drives fine for ten minutes then just starts erratic.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Absolutely, mechanical issues can sometimes fail sensors temporarily. Random action could stem from several sources including intermittent misfires that cause jerky hydraulic operation or a damaged oil cooler that sends unexpected signals to the sensors in charge of monitoring pressure. It's also possible that the erratic hydraulic performance itself could occasionally trip non-functional sensors, leading to no codes. Always keep an eye on other vehicle symptoms like engine vibrations or noises, as these could signify the root cause of the erratic behavior you’re witnessing. If the mechanical interference theory seems likely, thoroughly check for any anomalies in the engine compartment, accessory drive belts, and recently replaced or failed components. These areas could offer some clues to the irregular behavior before committing to more invasive diagnostics.",
